{"chunk_id": "db15736f387f", "text": "Vyasa consoles the grieving Yudhishthira by recounting how the sage Narada once restored King Srinjaya\u2019s slain son from hell, explaining that such a return was possible only because the youth had not fulfilled life\u2019s purposes. In contrast, Abhimanyu died heroically in battle after accomplishing a warrior\u2019s highest duty and has attained heavenly realms reserved for the righteous. Therefore the living should not grieve for the blessed dead. Comforted somewhat, Yudhishthira still worries about how to face Arjuna. As evening falls, Arjuna returns from battle after defeating the Samsaptakas but senses ominous signs and dread. Entering a silent, mournful camp, he realizes Abhimanyu is missing and launches into a long, anguished lament describing his son\u2019s virtues, beauty, and bravery, suspecting the boy died alone amid many enemies.\nArjuna returns from battle sensing ill omens and, finding the camp grief\u2011stricken, realizes Abhimanyu has fallen and laments his son in intense sorrow.\nThemes: parental grief, heroic death, omens and fate, war tragedy, heaven versus earthly loss", "vector": [0.005628687795251608, -0.019300304353237152, 0.000740191200748086, 0.005536749493330717, -0.011815818026661873, 0.011284616775810719, -0.01968167908489704, 0.00343918614089489, -0.016126718372106552, -0.012612619437277317, 0.012653481215238571, 0.009166623465716839, 0.013538816012442112, -0.0026491950266063213, -0.011958833783864975, 0.007075870409607887, 0.046119146049022675, 0.012776065617799759, 0.039090946316719055, -0.01650809310376644, -0.000267728726612404, 0.005867047235369682, -0.00808379054069519, 0.008002066984772682, -0.010378850623965263, 0.030292078852653503, 0.005066840443760157, -0.024421626701951027, 0.030537249520421028, -0.016862228512763977, -0.004637793637812138, -0.004865937866270542, -0.013354938477277756, -0.010133680887520313, -0.025266099721193314, 0.016603438183665276, 0.0008036118233576417, -0.0268